_factors": ["Polling average shows Talarico ahead by roughly 3 points in a state with a 6-point Republican lean", "Paxton carries heavy baggage, with $27 million in outside spending against him and weak fundraising", "Talarico's fundraising advantage ($68.6M raised, $21.5M on hand) and Democratic outside money flowing in", "Expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean D, with markets pricing Democrats near 60%"], "rationale": "The polling average puts Talarico up about 3 points, prediction markets price him near 60%, and most raters call the race a Tossup with several moving to Lean D. Paxton's financial disadvantage and the flood of anti-Paxton spending justify a modest Democratic edge over the state's Republican lean.", "watch": "Late Republican consolidation and outside spending against Talarico could close the polling gap in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: John Cornyn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Texas's open Senate seat has become one of the cycle's most competitive races. Attorney General Ken Paxton, who defeated incumbent John Cornyn in the Republican runoff, faces state Representative James Talarico. Recent polls from mid-to-late September consistently show Talarico narrowly ahead, with an average margin near 3 points, though several Republican-leaning pollsters show the race tied or close.\n\nTalarico holds a large financial edge, raising nearly $69 million to Paxton's $9 million, while outside groups have spent heavily attacking Paxton. Expert ratings are split between Tossup and Lean D, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a 60% chance. The race remains genuinely competitive given Texas's Republican-leaning fundamentals, but the weight of polling, money, and market pricing points to a modest Democratic advantage heading into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6697, "margin": 2.08}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6924, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 